---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { listPublicSnapshots } from "../../content/repository";
import { formatReviewDate } from "../../data/review-date";

export const prerender = false;
const snapshotResult = await listPublicSnapshots();
const currentReview = formatReviewDate(snapshotResult.snapshots[0]?.data.reviewed_at);
if (Astro.cache?.enabled) Astro.cache.set(snapshotResult.cacheHint);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Woodhouse works · Architecture"
  description="Independent projects, checked evidence, edited stories and a dated public record. See how work becomes a Woodhouse page, and who owns each part."
  path="/architecture/"
  image="/og/factory-workshop-20261018.jpg"
>
  <section class="container architecture-intro">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">WOODHOUSE</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span>Architecture</span>
    </nav>
    <p class="eyebrow">How Woodhouse works</p>
    <h1>
      A window into
      <br />
      the work.
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Woodhouse brings independently run games, music and tools into one place. We check the
      evidence, edit what is useful and publish a dated account that leads you to the real product.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container" aria-labelledby="publication-heading">
    <figure class="publication-flow">
      <figcaption>
        <h2 id="publication-heading">From project work to a public page</h2>
        <p>Review is the step that makes material ready to publish.</p>
      </figcaption>
      <ol class="flow-steps">
        <li>
          <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">
            01
          </span>
          <h3>Projects make things</h3>
          <p>Each product owns its experience, source, artwork and releases.</p>
          <span class="handoff">Evidence enters review</span>
        </li>
        <li class="review-step">
          <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">
            02
          </span>
          <h3>We check and edit</h3>
          <p>Check what actually works. Choose the story. Remove private material.</p>
          <span class="handoff">Explicit publication decision</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">
            03
          </span>
          <h3>Keep a public record</h3>
          <p>Approved summaries, evidence, media and review dates live in the editorial system.</p>
          <span class="handoff">Published content only</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">
            04
          </span>
          <h3>You read and explore</h3>
          <p>Woodhouse serves the page. Follow its links to try the independent product.</p>
          <span class="handoff">
            <a href="/projects/">Explore the projects →</a>
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="flow-boundary">
        <strong>Private inputs need review.</strong> An editor can derive a public-safe summary from
        private work. Original issue bodies, conversations and credentials stay outside public
        content; they have no direct publishing route.
      </p>
      <a class="flow-source" href="/diagrams/woodhouse-publication.d2">
        Editable technical diagram (D2 source) ↗
      </a>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container reading-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Who owns what?</p>
        <h2>Each part has a job.</h2>
        <p>
          Woodhouse connects the portfolio. Each game, station or tool keeps control of its own
          product and release decisions.
        </p>
        <a href="/factory/">See useful connections in the Factory →</a>
      </div>
      <dl class="roles">
        <div>
          <dt>The independent products</dt>
          <dd>
            The places you play, listen or create. Their sites own current product identity and
            artwork.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>GitHub</dt>
          <dd>
            The authority for source code, issues and pull requests. Many repositories are private;
            Woodhouse publishes reviewed summaries.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Editorial review &amp; EmDash</dt>
          <dd>
            Editors decide what is safe and useful to publish. EmDash, the content system, stores
            structured records and their publishing state.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Astro &amp; Cloudflare</dt>
          <dd>
            Astro supplies the page design. A Cloudflare Worker reads published records and renders
            the public page; static assets are delivered alongside it.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Your browser</dt>
          <dd>
            Displays the page and runs its local interactions. Following a product link takes you to
            that product's own experience.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section paper-section">
    <div class="container reading-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Reading the evidence</p>
        <h2>A date tells you what was checked.</h2>
        <p>
          {currentReview
            ? `The latest portfolio snapshot was reviewed ${currentReview}.`
            : "Each portfolio snapshot carries its own review date."}{" "}
          Project states belong to that review. Loading this page today does not verify a new
          release or move a project's review date forward.
        </p>
        <p>
          New evidence needs another editorial review. Earlier editions remain available as history.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="reading-notes">
        <h3>What visitors can access</h3>
        <p>
          Published pages, public evidence, approved images and read-only agent views. The live
          publication keeps the editorial office, authoring API and draft previews closed.
        </p>
        <h3>What the numbers mean</h3>
        <p>
          Analytics has a separate, limited collection endpoint. Its published aggregates explain
          their coverage; a click, a product visit and a useful action are different measurements.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/doctrine/">Read the publishing principles →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container technical-details">
      <h2>Under the page</h2>
      <p>The technical details are here when you need them.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>What happens at build time, and what happens when I visit?</summary>
        <div>
          <p>
            A code release builds the page templates, styles, scripts and static images. On a server
            render, Astro queries published EmDash records. Cached pages or query results may be
            reused, so a request does not always make a fresh database read.
          </p>
          <p>
            D1 stores editorial records. R2 stores approved editorial media. Existing analytics
            tables and their limited collection endpoint are separate from CMS authoring; D1 is not
            an empty or editorial-only database.
          </p>
          <p>
            This production release exposes the reading surfaces. Editorial access and publishing
            schedules are gated separately; installing a CMS does not mean its public authoring
            routes are open.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="https://docs.emdashcms.com/guides/querying-content/">
              EmDash's published-content and rendering contract ↗
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where do product images come from?</summary>
        <div>
          <p>
            Current product artwork follows the producer's own sharing metadata. A bounded
            request-time read checks approved product destinations and caches the result for five
            minutes. If that read fails, the last resolved image or reviewed record remains
            available. This presentation refresh does not change the reviewed project status.
          </p>
          <p>
            Optional Woodhouse campaign images and dossier illustrations keep their own credits. An
            image failure must not present a Woodhouse illustration as the producer's canonical art.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/share/">Browse the artwork and its sources →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What can the public agent view do?</summary>
        <div>
          <p>
            Agent Reception makes the same reviewed public information easier for software to read.
            It cannot query private repositories, run tools or publish an update. The authenticated
            editorial interface is a separate capability.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/agents/">Visit Agent Reception →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .architecture-intro {
    padding-block: 2.5rem 2rem;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--brass);
    font: 700 0.75rem var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .architecture-intro h1 {
    font: 700 clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5.75rem)/0.98 var(--display);
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .lead {
    max-width: 52rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 1.7vw, 1.35rem);
    color: var(--copy);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .publication-flow {
    margin: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-dark);
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 2rem);
  }
  .publication-flow figcaption {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .publication-flow h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font: 700 clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2.25rem)/1.15 var(--display);
  }
  .publication-flow figcaption p {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    color: var(--copy-soft);
  }
  .flow-steps {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    list-style: none;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .flow-steps li {
    min-width: 0;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--line-dark);
    padding-top: 1rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .flow-steps .review-step {
    border-color: var(--brass);
  }
  .step-number {
    font: 700 2.5rem/1 var(--display);
    color: var(--copy-soft);
  }
  .review-step .step-number {
    color: var(--brass);
  }
  .publication-flow h3 {
    font: 700 1.2rem/1.2 var(--display);
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .flow-steps p {
    color: var(--copy);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .handoff {
    margin-top: auto;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--copy-soft);
  }
  .handoff a {
    color: var(--brass);
  }
  .flow-boundary {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 1rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-dark);
    max-width: 70rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--copy);
  }
  .flow-boundary strong {
    color: var(--paper);
  }
  .flow-source {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--copy-soft);
  }
  .reading-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.3fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 6rem);
  }
  .reading-grid h2,
  .technical-details h2 {
    font: 700 clamp(2rem, 3vw, 3rem)/1.1 var(--display);
    margin: 0.7rem 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .reading-grid p,
  .roles dd,
  .technical-details p {
    color: var(--copy);
  }
  .roles {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .roles > div {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-dark);
    padding-block: 1rem;
  }
  .roles dt {
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .roles dd {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
  }
  .reading-notes h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .reading-notes h3:not(:first-child) {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .technical-details {
    max-width: 68rem;
  }
  .technical-details details {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-dark);
  }
  .technical-details summary {
    padding: 1.2rem 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .technical-details details > div {
    padding: 0 0 1rem;
    max-width: 54rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .flow-steps {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 2rem;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .architecture-intro {
      padding-top: 1.5rem;
    }
    .reading-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .flow-steps {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
    .flow-steps li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 2.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: 0.8rem;
    }
    .step-number {
      grid-row: span 3;
      font-size: 2rem;
    }
    .publication-flow h3 {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
    .flow-steps p {
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }
    .handoff {
      grid-column: 2;
    }
  }
</style>
